37
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:29,680
But we've got a lot to cover today, so let's get started, shall we?

38
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:34,940
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

39
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:44,080
If you watch football, and I really don't.

40
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:47,800
And I'm not a big Giant fan, but I've seen some games over time.

41
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:51,500
Watch a football game and somebody fumbles the ball.

42
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,600
And they're frustrated and they're, you know, they're kind of angry and they're disappointed.

43
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:09,100
And I think I have seen, if I remember correctly, you know, even a big strapping NFL player just about reduced to tears over having messed up a play.

44
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:10,580
Have you seen that?

45
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:11,220
I've seen it.

46
00:03:11,220 --> 00:03:18,720
And so, but, you know, their teammates get behind them and pat them on the back and dust them off and say, hey, we've got to get back out here and play.

47
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:21,900
So, you know, quit your crying and let's move on.

48
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:30,400
If big guys can be frustrated in that way, imagine what it is in the mind and the life of one of your youth leaders.

49
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:32,960
If he fumbles and he drops the ball.

50
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:35,580
It's a pretty humiliating experience.

51
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:38,500
Especially if you're a scout age boy.

52
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:42,480
Because you're very sensitive to what people think about you.

53
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:44,680
What your peers think about you.

54
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:47,940
What the adult leaders that you work with think about you.

55
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:52,240
And what the fellows who are younger than you think about you.

56
00:03:52,460 --> 00:04:01,640
So if a youth leader drops the ball or fumbles something or forgets to do something, he will either own up to the problem and deal with it directly.

57
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:03,820
Which would be the best case scenario.

58
00:04:04,040 --> 00:04:07,700
Hey, oh my gosh, I forgot to make that phone call.

59
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:10,820
Or I just got busy and I didn't do it.

60
00:04:10,980 --> 00:04:11,799
I am sorry.

61
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,260
That is a pretty rare reaction.

62
00:04:15,859 --> 00:04:22,780
Because it takes a lot of maturity and security to be able to just admit a mistake outright like that.

63
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:27,460
More often than not, they'll react in a couple of other ways.

64
00:04:27,460 --> 00:04:31,340
They will hide the mistake until somebody else finds it.

65
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:34,140
Or until they get pressed on it.

66
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:36,080
They won't just come in and say,

67
00:04:36,260 --> 00:04:38,740
Oh, oh, you know, oops, I blew it.

68
00:04:38,820 --> 00:04:39,020
Nope.

69
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:41,719
They'll hide it until somebody else figures it out.

70
00:04:41,960 --> 00:04:47,599
Then, they'll deny the responsibility or the importance of the task we're supposed to be doing.

71
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:52,200
And they'll make a lot of excuses and equivocations and things like that.

72
00:04:52,520 --> 00:04:56,479
Making some kind of a leadership misstep, just not carrying out a responsibility,

73
00:04:56,479 --> 00:04:58,620
is not as clear as fumbling a football.

74
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:01,060
I mean, you fumble a football, everybody sees it.

75
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:02,380
They see the ball on the ground and they say,

76
00:05:02,780 --> 00:05:03,900
Ooh, he fumbled the ball.

77
00:05:04,220 --> 00:05:09,680
But the result in the person's mind, I think, is just as dramatic.

78
00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:11,760
And they feel bad.

79
00:05:11,860 --> 00:05:13,080
And they feel frustrated.

80
00:05:13,460 --> 00:05:15,600
And they know that they've let everybody down.

81
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:16,500
So what happens?

82
00:05:16,940 --> 00:05:18,820
Well, as I said before,

83
00:05:18,820 --> 00:05:23,500
If a football gets fumbled, the whole team jumps for it, right?

84
00:05:24,140 --> 00:05:29,940
I mean, they don't stand there and point at the guy who fumbled the football and shake their fist at him and try and beat him up.

85
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:30,620
No.

86
00:05:30,880 --> 00:05:32,980
I mean, they all jump for the ball.

87
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:37,320
And then afterwards, they dust the guy off and pat him on the back and say,

88
00:05:37,860 --> 00:05:38,600
Oh, it's all right.

89
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:39,400
We'll get back at it.

90
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:40,719
And why do they do that?

91
00:05:40,720 --> 00:05:44,100
Because they know if they were carrying the ball, it's just as easy for them to fumble.

92
00:05:44,580 --> 00:05:46,520
That they're not immune from making a mistake.

93
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:54,620
Now, if the response to a fumble was that your team ostracized you and, you know, you got a sound beating,

94
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:58,680
Not many guys would want to carry a football under those conditions, would you think?

95
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:00,580
What about scouts?

96
00:06:01,080 --> 00:06:07,460
If they make mistakes, if we find fault and punish them, we don't treat them with empathy,

97
00:06:07,900 --> 00:06:10,900
and we don't dust them off and tell them to get back in the game,

98
00:06:11,160 --> 00:06:13,940
they're not going to want a position of responsibility.

99
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:18,300
Now, I'll overhear somebody discussing the idea that,

100
00:06:18,300 --> 00:06:24,400
Well, you know, we just can't get our older scouts to step up to responsible positions in the troop.

101
00:06:24,620 --> 00:06:33,820
The way you handle a fumble or a mistake could have a lot to do with the willingness of your scouts to take on responsibilities.

102
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:42,580
If they watch you ostracize and, you know, figuratively beat up on a scout who's made a mistake of some kind,

103
00:06:43,220 --> 00:06:47,640
they are not going to be interested in taking up a responsibility.

104
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:51,240
If we reply with empathy and encouragement,

105
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:54,740
they're going to be much more likely to take on those responsibilities.

106
00:06:55,460 --> 00:06:59,080
So it would be a good idea to make sure that, within your troop,

107
00:06:59,480 --> 00:07:01,640
you have a safe environment to make mistakes.

108
00:07:02,180 --> 00:07:06,200
Now, I'm not saying that scouts don't need to be accountable.

109
00:07:06,680 --> 00:07:11,400
We can accomplish that without dramatics or punishment or bad feelings.

110
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:35,179
This has to be the truth, folks,

111
00:07:35,180 --> 00:07:41,380
because there is no way anyone could make this up.

112
00:07:44,380 --> 00:07:46,480
All right, so, you know, you become a Boy Scout.

113
00:07:47,160 --> 00:07:49,560
And then you're going to go on your first camping trip,

114
00:07:49,660 --> 00:07:52,240
and you've got to patrol, and everything is cool,

115
00:07:52,460 --> 00:07:54,040
and then you find out you've got to cook.

116
00:07:55,060 --> 00:07:55,460
What?

117
00:07:56,400 --> 00:07:57,280
You've got to cook?

118
00:07:57,280 --> 00:08:05,299
Oh, yeah, the older guys say,

119
00:08:05,340 --> 00:08:06,719
oh, it's the best part of the trip.

120
00:08:06,840 --> 00:08:07,700
It's the most fun.

121
00:08:08,119 --> 00:08:08,919
It's great.

122
00:08:09,059 --> 00:08:10,840
You know, we can make anything that we want,

123
00:08:10,900 --> 00:08:13,919
and then the entire world opens up around you,

124
00:08:13,940 --> 00:08:16,539
and you start thinking of all the wonderful things that you can cook,

125
00:08:16,960 --> 00:08:20,659
and you figure out that I'm going to make hunter stew.

126
00:08:21,140 --> 00:08:21,880
Hunter stew.

127
00:08:21,880 --> 00:08:23,979
It just, it sounds wonderful.

128
00:08:23,980 --> 00:08:27,420
So, you find a recipe, and you make your list,

129
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:28,420
and you go to the grocery store,

130
00:08:28,440 --> 00:08:30,180
and you buy all these unfamiliar things,

131
00:08:30,280 --> 00:08:34,039
like chicken and potatoes and stuff like that.

132
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:37,159
I mean, things that, you know, you've seen around the house,

133
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:40,120
and you've seen maybe your mom or dad work with before,

134
00:08:40,260 --> 00:08:43,100
but in your hands, there's something magical

135
00:08:43,100 --> 00:08:45,120
and something new and exciting.

136
00:08:48,420 --> 00:08:51,860
So, you smile as you go to sleep that night and think,

137
00:08:51,860 --> 00:08:54,620
boy, the guys are going to be patting me on the back.

138
00:08:54,700 --> 00:08:57,220
Maybe they'll buy me one of those big white chef's hats, you know,

139
00:08:57,600 --> 00:09:00,120
after I make this hunter stew.

140
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:02,280
Oh, it's going to be grand.

141
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:08,440
Well, then you pack everything up,

142
00:09:08,480 --> 00:09:10,680
and you get on the camp out and all that stuff,

143
00:09:10,840 --> 00:09:14,300
and you're waiting all day, all day,

144
00:09:14,360 --> 00:09:16,080
until it's time to prepare dinner.

145
00:09:16,460 --> 00:09:18,520
You might even go back to the cooler

146
00:09:18,520 --> 00:09:20,579
and kind of fiddle around with the stuff

147
00:09:20,579 --> 00:09:22,380
and just make sure that it's all there

148
00:09:22,380 --> 00:09:23,319
and everything's ready.

149
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:25,920
And then somebody says,

150
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:27,860
well, it's about time we start cooking dinner.

151
00:09:28,079 --> 00:09:30,240
And so you turn around and bend down

152
00:09:30,240 --> 00:09:31,260
and grab a bag of potatoes,

153
00:09:31,420 --> 00:09:32,360
and you're about to hand them

154
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:34,020
to one of your fellow patrol members

155
00:09:34,020 --> 00:09:36,220
and say, well, we need to peel these potatoes, guys.

156
00:09:36,280 --> 00:09:38,199
And you look up, and they've disappeared.

157
00:09:38,520 --> 00:09:39,300
They're all gone.

158
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:43,780
They found things to do.

159
00:09:43,880 --> 00:09:47,380
One guy has decided that this is a good time

160
00:09:47,380 --> 00:09:48,780
to do tree identification.

161
00:09:49,900 --> 00:09:51,800
So he grabs his scout handbook

162
00:09:51,800 --> 00:09:52,859
and two other patrol members,

163
00:09:52,939 --> 00:09:53,880
and they head off into the woods.

164
00:09:53,959 --> 00:09:55,020
They're going to, you know,

165
00:09:55,040 --> 00:09:56,920
work on that tree identification thing

166
00:09:56,920 --> 00:09:58,520
for second class there.

167
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:02,859
And then, you know,

168
00:10:02,900 --> 00:10:03,900
you find out the other guys

169
00:10:03,900 --> 00:10:05,459
needed to go use a little latrine.

170
00:10:06,020 --> 00:10:07,079
It seems like they've been away

171
00:10:07,079 --> 00:10:08,359
for an awfully long time.

172
00:10:08,360 --> 00:10:09,980
But you look around,

173
00:10:10,060 --> 00:10:10,680
and there's your buddy.

174
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:12,780
And he's going to help you build the fire

175
00:10:12,780 --> 00:10:13,700
and get things going.

176
00:10:13,860 --> 00:10:15,120
So you start about 4.30,

177
00:10:15,180 --> 00:10:17,700
and then, oh, around 6.30 or so,

178
00:10:17,800 --> 00:10:18,780
you slice some potatoes

179
00:10:18,780 --> 00:10:20,040
into your hunter stew

180
00:10:20,040 --> 00:10:22,360
that's not really bubbling yet

181
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:23,180
over the fire.

182
00:10:25,780 --> 00:10:27,980
You know, the fire needs a little help.

183
00:10:28,140 --> 00:10:29,220
Well, finally, the patrol leader

184
00:10:29,220 --> 00:10:30,160
kind of figures out

185
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:31,320
that there's something wrong,

186
00:10:31,380 --> 00:10:32,820
and he gets all the patrol members,

187
00:10:32,920 --> 00:10:34,360
and now you have a roaring fire,

188
00:10:34,780 --> 00:10:37,200
and it's built up around your pot there,

189
00:10:37,200 --> 00:10:38,480
and your stew is boiling

190
00:10:38,480 --> 00:10:39,580
and everything,

191
00:10:39,720 --> 00:10:40,720
and it's only 7.30.

192
00:10:41,120 --> 00:10:42,460
I mean, you've got plenty of time.

193
00:10:42,940 --> 00:10:44,460
And so the water boils

194
00:10:44,460 --> 00:10:45,860
and everything looks great,

195
00:10:46,060 --> 00:10:47,660
and the, oh, my,

196
00:10:47,880 --> 00:10:49,800
the aroma coming off of that stew.

197
00:10:50,020 --> 00:10:51,440
Oh, it's just amazing.

198
00:10:51,820 --> 00:10:52,940
Just amazing.

199
00:10:52,940 --> 00:10:57,880
At around 8 o'clock,

200
00:10:57,960 --> 00:10:59,420
after the fire is burned out

201
00:10:59,420 --> 00:11:01,120
and the guys are running off

202
00:11:01,120 --> 00:11:01,660
into the woods

203
00:11:01,660 --> 00:11:02,720
to find more firewood

204
00:11:02,720 --> 00:11:03,460
to reheat the stew,

205
00:11:03,520 --> 00:11:05,100
you test a couple of pieces of potato,

206
00:11:05,220 --> 00:11:06,740
and they're still hard as a rock.

207
00:11:07,400 --> 00:11:07,960
Oh, well.

208
00:11:08,180 --> 00:11:10,120
It'll only take another half hour or so.

209
00:11:10,780 --> 00:11:11,480
You hope.

210
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:17,700
So the patrol finally gets desperate,

211
00:11:17,700 --> 00:11:19,900
and they decide at 8.30,

212
00:11:20,200 --> 00:11:21,200
you know, we're going to eat

213
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:22,780
because we're starving to death.

214
00:11:22,980 --> 00:11:24,220
My God, we didn't know

215
00:11:24,220 --> 00:11:25,380
this was going to take so long.

216
00:11:25,800 --> 00:11:27,300
So you dish out the stew,

217
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:29,500
and everybody gets a big mess kit

218
00:11:29,500 --> 00:11:30,420
full of stew,

219
00:11:30,580 --> 00:11:31,780
and they all sit around,

220
00:11:32,160 --> 00:11:33,760
and they're chomping on it,

221
00:11:33,860 --> 00:11:36,140
and they have to saw at it hard

222
00:11:36,140 --> 00:11:38,160
with a knife to break it up

223
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:39,520
and to get it into pieces

224
00:11:39,520 --> 00:11:41,020
so that it will fit in their mouth,

225
00:11:41,060 --> 00:11:42,100
and then you've got to chew on it

226
00:11:42,100 --> 00:11:43,420
for 10 or 15 minutes

227
00:11:43,420 --> 00:11:45,080
before you can get it down.

228
00:11:45,080 --> 00:11:47,380
And everybody kind of looks at each other,

229
00:11:47,540 --> 00:11:48,280
and you say,

230
00:11:48,380 --> 00:11:49,380
well, how's the stew?

231
00:11:50,020 --> 00:11:50,540
And they say,

232
00:11:50,620 --> 00:11:52,080
eh, it's all right.

233
00:11:59,200 --> 00:12:00,760
So you figure the next time,

234
00:12:01,120 --> 00:12:01,780
the next time,

235
00:12:01,940 --> 00:12:03,900
it's going to be some other sucker's time

236
00:12:03,900 --> 00:12:04,640
to, you know,

237
00:12:04,700 --> 00:12:06,020
buy the food and cook.

238
00:12:06,360 --> 00:12:07,360
I'm not going to deal

239
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:08,540
with these guys anymore.

240
00:12:09,180 --> 00:12:09,740
Oh, no.

241
00:12:10,060 --> 00:12:11,700
I'm not going to mess around with them.

242
00:12:12,280 --> 00:12:13,800
Go and work my heart out

243
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:14,839
and make stew for them,

244
00:12:14,920 --> 00:12:15,560
and they don't even,

245
00:12:15,680 --> 00:12:16,800
they don't even like it.

246
00:12:19,560 --> 00:12:20,800
Now, secret is,

247
00:12:20,839 --> 00:12:21,880
you don't really like it either.

248
00:12:22,520 --> 00:12:22,819
You know,

249
00:12:22,920 --> 00:12:24,520
salt and pepper would have been a good idea,

250
00:12:24,599 --> 00:12:24,959
but of course,

251
00:12:25,020 --> 00:12:26,000
you didn't bring any along.

252
00:12:26,640 --> 00:12:27,140
And then,

253
00:12:27,420 --> 00:12:30,020
then the light shines down from heaven,

254
00:12:30,260 --> 00:12:32,240
and it comes into your mind

255
00:12:32,240 --> 00:12:34,180
that there's something else in the cooler.

256
00:12:34,180 --> 00:12:39,459
So you go to the cooler,

257
00:12:39,560 --> 00:12:41,040
you grab that box full of popsicles.

